  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2023 scholarly book is showing early signs of active use, with 2,035 recorded downloads from the OAPEN platform and open-access availability through a gold OA model. Its record also shows institutional support from KU Leuven, while citation evidence remains at zero at this stage, suggesting reach is currently stronger than formal scholarly citation uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Islamophobia as a Form of Radicalisation is an interdisciplinary edited volume published by Leuven University Press that examines Islamophobia as both a conceptual debate and a sociopolitical reality. The book brings together research spanning media analysis, ethnography, action research, and advocacy across several European countries and Canada, which is reflected in its broad conceptual profile across political science, media studies, sociology, law, history, and related fields.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is usage rather than citation. The book has accumulated 2,035 downloads via OAPEN, indicating that readers are actively accessing the work in its open-access form. That pattern is consistent with a title intended to circulate across academic and public-interest audiences, especially on a topic that intersects scholarship, policy, and public debate.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book’s open availability is reinforced by repository evidence showing an openly accessible instance, and its inclusion in a named series suggests an identifiable scholarly framing within a larger thematic collection. Funding support from KU Leuven is also documented, which provides further institutional context for the publication.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record is still sparse, with no OpenAlex citations captured in the current digest. That does not diminish the book’s relevance; rather, it indicates that its measurable scholarly uptake is still at an early stage or not yet reflected in citation-based systems. In this record, the main story is one of access, discoverability, and active reading, rather than established citation impact.</p>",
